                <h2>1. How should accounts be tiered?</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Should a multi-account team organize accounts by business line or by ad platform?</strong></p>
<p>Do not organize them by platform alone. A practical structure has at least three tiers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core tier:</strong> Proven products, stable creatives, and primary budgets. Keep permissions lean and operational changes limited.</li>
<li><strong>Testing tier:</strong> New products, creatives, regions, and audiences. Allow frequent adjustments, but enforce strict spend limits for each account.</li>
<li><strong>Isolation tier:</strong> Accounts associated with policy concerns, unstable payments, rejected creatives, or activities that need to be suspended without being permanently closed.</li>
</ul>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team initially mixed brands, regions, and platforms within one flat structure. As test-account spending increased, the team could not determine whether overspending came from strategy or account quality. It later introduced a consistent “brand-region-maturity” structure and isolated risky accounts, making budget decisions much clearer.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How should accounts be named?</strong></p>
<p>Use fixed fields: <code>region-product line-stage-account status</code>, such as <code>US-Tool A-Stable-Normal</code>. Avoid vague names such as “Account 1” and “Account 2,” or embedding frequently changing owner information. A standardized naming system reduces handover, payment, and reconciliation errors.</p>
<h2>2. How should budgets, payments, and permissions work together?\n</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Should core and test accounts share the same payment account?</strong></p>
<p>Not entirely. Core accounts should have stable, independent funding cycles. Test accounts should operate with limited balances or small top-ups, while isolated accounts should receive no new funding. If several teams share one balance, it becomes difficult to trace a sudden spending spike to a specific payment order.</p>
<p>Implement three controls for each account:</p>
<ol>
<li>A daily spending cap to prevent abnormal consumption;</li>
<li>A per-payment limit to control testing costs;</li>
<li>A balance alert threshold so the owner can replenish funds in time.</li>
</ol>
<p>Thresholds should reflect product margin, average order value, and cash-flow cycles rather than a universal percentage.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Should media buyers have permission to recharge accounts and change account-level settings?</strong></p>
<p>Generally, no. Separate responsibilities by role. Buyers can create ads, adjust budgets, and replace creatives. Supervisors approve budget increases and account activation or suspension. Finance manages payments, invoices, and refunds. Administrators handle authorization, asset transfers, and incidents. High-risk actions should be logged with the operator, timestamp, before-and-after values, and reason.</p>
<h2>3. How can one account put the entire budget at risk?</h2>
<p><strong>Q: When an account behaves abnormally, should the team stop it immediately or monitor it first?</strong></p>
<p>The response should depend on the type of anomaly. A sudden spending surge, untraceable conversions, or repeated payment failures warrants an immediate budget freeze and funding restriction. Creative rejection issues may first be addressed by replacing the creative and reducing exposure before monitoring recovery. Historical performance is not a reason to continue funding an abnormal account.</p>
<p>Create an account health checklist covering approval status, balance, payment method, login environment, authorization ownership, and recent spending changes. Every exception should have an owner and a resolution deadline.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How can teams prevent creative duplication and internal competition across accounts?</strong></p>
<p>The creative library should record eligible accounts, regions, audiences, performance stage, and usage rights. Proven creatives can be copied into comparable accounts for testing, but they should not be distributed mechanically across overlapping audiences. Testing accounts should handle exploration, while core accounts scale validated plans. Otherwise, the core tier becomes an uncontrolled testing environment.</p>
<h2>4. How should reconciliation be scheduled?</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Does finance need to check every account every day?</strong></p>
<p>Not usually. Reconcile core accounts daily, summarize test accounts weekly, and review isolated accounts separately. Daily checks should focus on three issues: differences between top-ups and platform spending, consistency between account balances and spendable balances, and suspicious charges or duplicate payments.</p>
<p>Maintain a mapping from accounts to campaigns and billing statements, and retain payment orders, platform invoices, exchange rates, and refund records. As a team expands, the main challenge is often not campaign optimization but attributing financial consumption correctly. Account tiering becomes operational only when budgets, permissions, creative controls, and reconciliation are connected.</p>
